        <div style="border:1px solid #DFE3E9; border-radius:6px; padding:20px;">
          <div style="font-size:17.5px; font-weight:700; color:#16234F; margin-bottom:8px;">What are the eyesight requirements to pass a taxi medical?</div>
          <div style="font-size:15px; color:#3E4453; line-height:1.6;">
            Under statutory DVLA Group 2 rules, you must achieve visual acuity of at least 6/7.5 on a Snellen chart in your better eye and at least 6/60 in the other eye (with glasses or contact lenses if worn). Your horizontal visual field must be at least 160 degrees.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div style="border:1px solid #DFE3E9; border-radius:6px; padding:20px;">
          <div style="font-size:17.5px; font-weight:700; color:#16234F; margin-bottom:8px;">What happens if my blood pressure is high on the day?</div>
          <div style="font-size:15px; color:#3E4453; line-height:1.6;">
            To pass under Group 2 criteria, blood pressure must be consistently below 180/100 mmHg. If your initial reading is elevated due to white-coat stress, our doctors will let you rest for 10 to 15 minutes and take up to three repeat readings before any decision is made.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
